#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128862 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5d1d859-0b86-5507-a7bf-3d805a85adf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/id #uuid "674e7733-7a8e-5804-a867-82c74c583dcc" :question/math-academy-id "e-4687" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128863 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d390841-351e-54f5-ad19-90b6ebae5799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/id #uuid "b6e4983b-c695-5944-9637-6d7547dae849" :question/math-academy-id "e-4688" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128864 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0050b2-b5bc-54a6-aeaf-94cd5d7e5dfb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Operations That Would Transform a Matrix To Row Echelon Form"}] :question/id #uuid "97237f8e-b317-5c86-86d1-8fd3cc83e66b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4689" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that by replacing $a_{32}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128930 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31c3678b-4f00-54d6-bb84-c7718fd622cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Leading Entries of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "7fd5c01f-c20e-5c47-b8d9-8cfbe634c14d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6166" :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-1$, $-4$ and $8$, respectively.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-1+(-4)+8=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5d1d859-0b86-5507-a7bf-3d805a85adf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83249f1a-806e-5812-86cc-72f7bc600738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304455} {:answer/id #uuid "8c16afb6-fe97-5c65-b4bb-374c739a8eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304456} {:answer/id #uuid "aec68487-4012-5c37-b5d8-45c7d56287a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304457} {:answer/id #uuid "c99f9ddd-497a-5f85-8af2-bdc0e0ab93e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304458} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc9e24c-a2e1-55c0-9a57-2df98e32d04d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fc9e24c-a2e1-55c0-9a57-2df98e32d04d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304459} :answer-field/id #uuid "44327163-ea32-5fe7-82ec-596bf0f8278a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18b4c8df-6376-5360-8583-1732c8739004" :question/math-academy-id "q-129232" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -4 & 5 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since ${a}_{33}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not in row echelon form since the third row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since ${c}_{33}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -4 & 5 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are **not** in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0050b2-b5bc-54a6-aeaf-94cd5d7e5dfb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Operations That Would Transform a Matrix To Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "599d420f-68dc-548f-8100-ee350545b9c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{12}$ and ${a}_{14}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304462} {:answer/id #uuid "28f8fc8d-5b03-5a78-b6fb-d6201e6a8be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{22}$, ${a}_{23}$ and ${a}_{33}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304463} {:answer/id #uuid "471c5e6e-d253-59a7-b2f7-337a94ee5052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{22}$ and ${a}_{33}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304464} {:answer/id #uuid "1461240c-50c6-566c-a093-3b1f5f697cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{22}$, ${a}_{33}$ and ${a}_{44}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304465} {:answer/id #uuid "2e84fb75-1e79-5759-b113-883e5275cc6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{33}$ and ${a}_{44}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1461240c-50c6-566c-a093-3b1f5f697cb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{22}$, ${a}_{33}$ and ${a}_{44}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304465} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d744ac4-6acc-5396-9500-e1067d26db08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18c5c4bf-9773-53a5-af29-8e4d5e1f1556" :question/math-academy-id "q-129423" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{22}$, ${a}_{33}$ and ${a}_{44}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\,⟶\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5d1d859-0b86-5507-a7bf-3d805a85adf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efc1bc5a-432e-567a-bfa6-55e3ea8764f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128043} {:answer/id #uuid "f96bf16b-376d-5cdc-ad60-e9b42587d659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128044} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6d79cd-7045-5677-87b2-8158e24b7b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128045} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2509ee-b2b7-5cf8-806f-7368390720a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128046} {:answer/id #uuid "30d2d8bb-8dc8-5393-add1-e1fa0317c726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a2509ee-b2b7-5cf8-806f-7368390720a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128046} :answer-field/id #uuid "772a74c8-11f6-5eb6-adf9-b4536f14c368" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128042}] :question/id #uuid "658776f4-d49a-5e5d-a10c-50a3db34ad1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-42006" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129709 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0050b2-b5bc-54a6-aeaf-94cd5d7e5dfb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Operations That Would Transform a Matrix To Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c8e22f3-676a-5a92-a61b-bc2c0eb73092" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{21}$,  ${a}_{33}$, and ${a}_{44}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304468} {:answer/id #uuid "32a2d068-8416-5a76-af1f-23a5efef8c9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{22}$ and ${a}_{33}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304469} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c3cb60-97c6-54f7-8114-6b9fae0b26e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{21}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304470} {:answer/id #uuid "c87ebfd1-ded6-5601-b4c8-b96c738cc2b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{43}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304471} {:answer/id #uuid "174f82ae-47f5-5915-a43e-867e0a314d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{21}$ and ${a}_{43}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "174f82ae-47f5-5915-a43e-867e0a314d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{21}$ and ${a}_{43}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304472} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a34e302-a81e-5a13-99fb-06cd3e097642" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ab2c24d-7d61-5976-acd1-acab7ad4094a" :question/math-academy-id "q-42008" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{21}$ and ${a}_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⟶\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31c3678b-4f00-54d6-bb84-c7718fd622cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Leading Entries of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb93c99f-65f0-574d-9bbd-005c2a934a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304524} {:answer/id #uuid "760bf893-a58a-56f3-ad83-4718cb8f84ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304525} {:answer/id #uuid "5a192ef9-2a90-5972-b413-5992a8f83b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304526} {:answer/id #uuid "14ec15f6-2062-5f06-ac6f-790c4aaadedd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304527} {:answer/id #uuid "5197e390-8365-5e16-95c7-e25c10a6deca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14ec15f6-2062-5f06-ac6f-790c4aaadedd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304527} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1a325a9-0c43-5d0d-ad7a-fe4c5f98f8e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6278ef30-11c3-5778-87e0-53c832948da5" :question/math-academy-id "q-73148" :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd rows of $A$ are $-7$, $5$, and $3$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-7+5+3=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0050b2-b5bc-54a6-aeaf-94cd5d7e5dfb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Operations That Would Transform a Matrix To Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a387be7-78a7-5c2d-a12d-dfa681a7fd9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{32}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304531} {:answer/id #uuid "4241414e-7c40-523a-87e3-5292db20500b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{32}$ and ${a}_{41}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304532} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fe7a11-4496-560c-be3d-4f66cb3213f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{32},$ ${a}_{33},$ and ${a}_{41}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304533} {:answer/id #uuid "ee029320-01b9-525e-99e4-5356a1bf8a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{32}$ only ${a}_{33}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304534} {:answer/id #uuid "24246ec3-e3ea-5a7b-a084-9b5fe3985b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{33}$ and ${a}_{41}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9fe7a11-4496-560c-be3d-4f66cb3213f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{32},$ ${a}_{33},$ and ${a}_{41}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304533} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f1dd443-83c4-5c39-9b5b-270749ba24ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7cd248c8-ca36-5b5b-84ff-ea9f7200cda2" :question/math-academy-id "q-73151" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that by replacing ${a}_{32},$ ${a}_{33},$ and ${a}_{41}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,⟶\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form."}]}
